course: dictionary
lesson: 13078
title: Made-up and false
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some stories are made up. The people, places and events in them did not really happen, and the word for this is fictitious. «The dragon in the book is fictitious.» There is no real dragon, so the author invented it.
explain: Fictitious has a second meaning too. «The thief gave a fictitious name to the police.» Here it means a false name, made up to trick people. Look at the clues: a thief is trying to hide who he really is.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Does «The dragon in the book is made up» still make sense? It does, so "made up" is a good meaning for fictitious. Always test your idea this way.
fact: Fictitious is an adjective, so it describes a noun such as a character, a name or a place.
fact: «Fictitious» means invented or made up; not real or true.
fact: «Fictitious» can also mean a false name or detail used to cheat people.
fact: Phrases you will meet are fictitious character, fictitious name and entirely fictitious.
fact: A story that is not true is called fiction, and the people in it are often fictitious.
exercise: «Captain Mossbeard is a fictitious pirate who sails a sky-blue ship.» What does fictitious mean here? | *Made up and not real | Very old and very rare | Famous and loved by everyone | Living in the next village
exercise: «The thief gave a fictitious address to the bank.» What does fictitious suggest? | A very long address with many numbers | A kind address with a big garden | *A false address, made up to trick people | A real address, written neatly on the form
exercise: «The witch in the story is fictitious, so she never lived.» Find and copy one word that means made up. | lived | story | witch | *fictitious
test: «The author invented a fictitious town called Greyhaven.» What is fictitious describing? | A town where the author lives | A town full of happy people | A town that is very big | *A town that is not real
test: «He used a fictitious name to sign the letter.» Why might someone use a fictitious name? | *To hide who they really are and trick people | Because the name was very short | To make the letter longer | To say thank you to a friend
exam: «The detective soon found out that the customer's name was entirely fictitious.» What does entirely fictitious mean? | Written in a very neat way | Very famous and well known | *Completely made up and false | Partly true but a bit changed
